gative or closed findings include the original H5 direction for KEAP1 loss/NFE2L2, H2 BRAF V600E stronger BRAF dependency, H10 MYC amplification stronger RPL11 dependency, H11 ARID1A loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H12 KRAS mutation weaker RIT1 dependency, and now H3 EGFR amplification stronger EGFR dependency. Several associations remain unresolved but low priority: H1 KRAS mutation KRAS dependency, H4 ERBB2 amplification ERBB2 dependency, H6 TP53 loss MDM2 dependency, and H9 MYC amplification POLR2A dependency; their current estimates are small or compatible with modest effects but do not justify immediate follow-up over the accepted large effects. The most informative next analyses are context and robustness checks for the accepted large effects. H15 already tests SMARCA4 loss in adenocarcinoma; I additionally propose an adenocarcinoma-versus-complement interaction to determine whether the SMARCA2 effect is context-specific. I also propose MSI-high WRN dependency within the dominant Avana CRISPR library and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 dependency within the Avana library to test whether the accepted large effects remain present in the main assay library context. If these follow-ups are robust, the dataset-level conclusions will be strengthened; if they show heterogeneity, the accepted effects may need to be qualified as context-specific rather than general within this dataset.",
